# Ubuntu Qt-owned session

Status: the live Qt runtime is integrated. The operator accepted the
1.1.044-ubuntu-qt-session playback repair and reports working mouse/tablet
input. That candidate retains the cursor-texture ownership repair, uses
ordinary 2D textures for the Intel EGLImage import path, and passed the hosted
compile, component, compositor, sanitizer, launcher and package gates.
Complete profile, input-recovery and audio/video soak qualification remains
separate from that acceptance.

The accepted 1.1.045 follow-up improves unpinned toolbar access after switching
to a window. It uses a twelve-logical-pixel hover strip below the native title
bar, with the existing one-second dwell and Pin behavior. Fullscreen retains
its three-pixel trigger. No extra reveal button or custom title bar is added.
Hosted compilation, compositor tests at 100%/125%, sanitizers and packaging
passed. The separate Qt DEB is downloaded and the operator confirmed windowed
reveal works. Candidate 1.1.046 restores stacked stats and stateful
pin/fullscreen vector icons without changing that interaction. The operator
also approved continuous automatic recovery with a Disconnect-only status
dialog, retaining explicit auto-disconnect and terminal security failures.
The Qt timeout now reveals a notice rather than pausing retry requests.
Hosted qualification and packaging passed, and the separate .046 DEB is
downloaded. All 38 window/input cases pass offscreen, with sanitizers and on
GNOME/Wayland at 100%/125%; both complete Client builds and package isolation
also pass. Those UI renders used the default style, missing the production
Material theme's edge padding/insets. The operator reported squeezed icons.
Current candidate 1.1.047 removes that style dependency from the toolbar buttons:
centered 24-pixel vectors, 32-pixel invisible hit targets, and hover/press-only
backgrounds. Minimize/close now match the other vectors. The test/probe style
matches production Material Dark/Dense, with explicit geometry, rendering and
hit-target regressions. Hosted run39355378111 passes all40 window/input cases
offscreen, under sanitizers and on GNOME100/125, both complete app builds and
package gates. The .047 DEB is downloaded with verified provenance/hashes.
The test/probe explicitly finalize Fontconfig after Qt teardown; leak detection
remains enabled without suppressions. Subsequent operator gates remain toolbar
and cable-interruption testing: restore the network without
pressing a dialog button and confirm automatic video/input recovery. See
HANDOFF for exact source, package hashes and remaining hardware gates.

The operator accepted .047's toolbar. Candidate .048 hides the decoder's
startup test image while preserving the real exact-format draw and swap check.
Marked qualification frames are cleared to black in the same GPU command
stream before controls/swap; live frames retain their existing path. The
connecting message waits for real live presentation, with generation-scoped
readiness. Controlled-feedback and compositor pixel regressions cover the
transition, reconnect generation changes and precision rejection. Hosted run
39358327903 passed both complete builds and the package gates, all nine presenter
and 40 window/input cases, sanitizers, and 72 compositor conceal/reveal pixel
checks across 100%/125% and window modes. The separate .048 DEB and reports are
downloaded with verified hashes/provenance. Neither Host nor ordinary Client is
changed. Repeated live connection/reconnection acceptance remains the next
operator check; hosted software rendering is not Intel hardware qualification.

The longstanding frozen top-left arrow also reproduces with PLANK closed:
local pen movement clears it, and opening a standard GNOME dialog restores it.
Disabling Qt's tablet interface did not help. No input workaround is shipped;
the exact local compositor/driver defect remains unproven.
Implementation started on `ubuntu-qt-session`, from parent
`1572c09e26d6ce7db20d9d1feefe11a3155963b8` / Client
`58f4df097c1cdf11bf6b4b429701f156e2b10109` (`client-session-recovery`).
Refreshed with main `38e00a557670df063926af1ef3466156145302ae`, the 1.1.039
release baseline. The .036 Wacom and .037 lifecycle repairs and persistent-tablet
Host integration are retained. The independent CUDA candidate is not included.
This does not merge the Qt experiment into main or qualify it for shipping.

## Scope and decisions

The operator has no identifiable Development NUC available for this experiment.
Continue isolated development/testing on GitHub-hosted Ubuntu Actions; record Intel GPU,
physical input and live-session gates as pending. Do not contact old end-user
addresses or infer hardware acceptance from software-rendered builder tests.
When a usable Client candidate is ready, the operator will install and test it.

The experimental DEB must be co-installable with the regular Client. Use a
distinct `plank-client-qt` package/executable and **PLANK Client Qt** launcher,
separate desktop/application identity, private library/resource paths, settings,
bookmarks/trust store and logs. Do not replace or conflict with `plank-client`,
share mutable settings, overwrite `/etc/plank/client.conf`, or register a second
handler for the production URL scheme. Package-file overlap, installation and
removal independence are explicit gates before distributing the experiment.
This is a testing identity, not a rename of the production product.

Ubuntu 26.04 only. Leave the macOS Client, both Hosts, protocol, decoder policy,
audio synchronization and raw-HID Wacom implementation unchanged. Qt owns the
session window and input on its GUI thread. Workers must not create, destroy or
dispatch events on that window. Do not wrap a Qt-owned native surface in an SDL
window or retain two event dispatchers for the same surface.

Use one Qt Quick scene per output: video underlay, Host-authoritative tablet
cursor, toolbar and recovery prompt. A normal Qt raster top-level owns window
decorations; the scene is its Qt-owned native child. Video never passes through
the raster backing store or Qt Wayland's 8-bit decoration framebuffer. Qt owns
both native lifetimes and their shared input hierarchy; no manual Wayland
surface/queue dispatch is added. Toolbar input stays in window-local logical
coordinates, independently of the video viewport and letterboxing. No cursor
warps, global-pointer polling or second native toolbar window. Remote drag
ownership lasts through its matching release even when crossing the toolbar;
local toolbar drags must never reach the Host. Focus loss, geometry replacement,
disconnect and teardown release held remote input.

The renderer provides its exact source/destination rectangles in pixels. Input
maps Qt logical coordinates through that same viewport; do not reconstruct a
second aspect-fit transform from the negotiated Host dimensions. Each output
uses its actual backing size, including fractional scale. Window resizing must
not renegotiate Host resolution.

## Ordered implementation gates

The operator requires feature equivalence, not a reduced demonstration. The
following live-runtime checklist is a completion gate; component tests alone
do not check these boxes. Reuse the established transport, authentication,
decoder-policy, audio-clock and tablet implementations behind UI-independent
boundaries rather than maintaining a second implementation of those contracts.

- [ ] Asynchronous connect, cancel, takeover, disconnect and application exit;
      Qt continues processing input throughout startup, recovery and shutdown.
- [ ] Exact-profile hardware-first decode, software fallback, 8/10-bit identity
      and full-range YCbCr presentation; retained GPU resources and bounded queues.
- [ ] Fullscreen/windowed, minimize/restore, one or several outputs, native and
      scaled-span presentation, fractional scale, and one shared input transform.
- [ ] Complete toolbar: live drag/pin, bitrate acknowledgement, rate/loss/RTT,
      microphone, camera, fullscreen, minimize, disconnect and stats overlay.
- [ ] Keyboard and system-shortcut policy, absolute mouse/buttons/wheel,
      clipboard, Host cursor shapes, and Host-authoritative tablet cursor.
- [ ] Unchanged raw-HID and normalized Wacom forwarding, focus release,
      reconnect generations, pressure, tip/buttons and Tablet Margins.
- [ ] Existing audio playback/synchronization, microphone/camera forwarding and
      device selection, including hotplug and shutdown without retained devices.
- [ ] Host timeout/automatic recovery/Disconnect, GDM/desktop handoff, desktop-lock recovery,
      network interruption and decoder recovery without an unresponsive UI.
- [ ] Separately installable real Qt Client candidate, production-file/state
      isolation, GitHub-hosted clean build, regression and package-content gates.
- [ ] Operator hardware acceptance and moving-video/audio soak; explicitly
      pending until an authorized physical Client is available.

Do not stop after individual component checkpoints while implementation can
continue without operator input. Do not remove the packaging guard until the
live runtime is connected, or describe hosted software-GPU tests as hardware
acceptance. The ordinary Client remains the behavior and rollback reference.

1. Build the real Qt window/input/toolbar component in an isolated, non-networked
   qualification executable. Exercise edge reveal (1 second), hide (5 seconds),
   pinning, live dragging, keyboard/button release, letterbox, fractional scale,
   mixed output slices and unresponsive-Host UI. No application package changes
   or fabricated remote-session/performance claims at this stage.
2. Adapt the existing exact-format GPU presenters to the Qt-owned surface.
   Prefer direct scene-graph render-node/underlay rendering rather than an extra
   intermediate image. Preserve VA-API Y410/XR30 identity and software FFmpeg
   planar precision; qualify all 1024 levels, full range and channel identity.
   A requested 10-bit surface is not proof that the actual swapchain is 10-bit.
   Fail qualification on an 8-bit substitution. No video QImage/readback path.
3. Move Ubuntu session lifecycle to asynchronous Qt GUI orchestration. Retain
   transport/decoder workers, bounded queues and .037 callback retirement.
   Reuse SDL/PipeWire audio initially, without SDL video/event ownership. Wire
   live stats, acknowledged bitrate, microphone/camera, recovery and disconnect.
4. Attach unchanged raw-HID/normalized tablet transport to the new UI owner.
   Mouse uses the compositor cursor; tablet uses Host-authoritative position
   and cursor shape within the same Qt scene. Never infer tablet position from
   the mouse or send synthetic mouse movement to align a cursor.
5. Qualify on an unoccupied authorized Development NUC: fullscreen/windowed,
   100% and 125%, one/two unequal monitors, custom cursor, hover/tip/buttons,
   pressure and Flame Tablet Margins, network loss, reconnect, Host reboot,
   minimize/restore, repeated teardown and moving-video/audio soak. Compare
   CPU, latency and frame-drop behavior against 1.1.039 using identical profiles.
6. Only after those gates, make the Ubuntu implementation the default and
   remove superseded Ubuntu SDL-video/manual Wayland UI code. Keep macOS paths.
   Build a branch-labelled candidate; no merge or release without acceptance.

## Code findings behind this order

`Session::exec()` currently waits for the SDL worker after initial launch,
blocking normal Qt GUI dispatch for the active Linux stream. The SDL EGL
renderer can recreate native Wayland surfaces during backend selection. Custom
toolbar and tablet-cursor objects then need parent-surface rebuilding. Qt
window ownership cannot be substituted safely by changing one creation call.

The first component deliberately has no transport or decoder dependency. Its
signals define the UI-to-session boundary, not a new wire protocol. Shipping
build files must not enable it before the renderer/lifecycle gates. Retain the
existing production path intact for baseline comparison and rollback.

## References

- https://doc.qt.io/qt-6.10/qquickwindow.html
- https://doc.qt.io/qt-6.10/qtquick-visualcanvas-scenegraph.html
- https://doc.qt.io/qt-6.10/qsgrendernode.html
- https://doc.qt.io/qt-6.10/qwindow.html

Qt documents GUI/render-thread separation and native GPU integration. Those
APIs enable the approach; they do not establish performance or color fidelity.

## First qualification findings

The following sections retain staged qualification evidence, not the current
implementation backlog. Early statements that live runtime or packaging was
not connected describe those checkpoints. Current implementation and package
state are summarized in `HANDOFF.md` and the final section below.

The isolated component passes 24 QtTest checks on the Ubuntu 26.04 / Qt 6.10.2
builder, both offscreen and on an isolated GNOME/Wayland compositor. The same
24 checks pass with ASan/UBSan and leak detection enabled. Mouse events in this
suite are injected at Qt's event boundary, not physical seat or Wacom input.
No remote session or hardware acceptance is implied.

The precision gate deliberately tests the *bound framebuffer*, not just the
reported OpenGL context format. A directly decorated Qt Quick window reports a
10-bit context but uses an R8/G8/B8 content framebuffer on the test compositor.
That gate fails as intended. The initial fullscreen test inspected the first
frame before compositor configuration settled; a later-frame test correctly
finds an R10/G10/B10 fullscreen framebuffer. Do not repeat the earlier inference
that settled fullscreen also uses the 8-bit content buffer.

The proposed `QtSessionShell` preserves ordinary window decorations around a
native Qt Quick child. Windowed and fullscreen tests, including four mode
transitions before readback, retain every code in all four 1024-level
red/green/blue/gray ramps. The windowed gate also checks that the top-level title
bar still has a nonzero frame margin. These checks pass at 100% and with forced
`QT_SCALE_FACTOR=1.25`, without a Qt patch, HDR, or disabling window decorations.
The 24 component checks also pass under that forced scale. This is not a real
compositor fractional-scale or mixed-monitor hardware acceptance test.

The virtual builder uses Mesa llvmpipe; these are precision/lifecycle feasibility
checks, not GPU throughput, panel or decoded-video qualification. No decoded
video frame has traversed the new component. The EGL/VA-API adapter described
below compiles but its real import/decoder path remains unqualified. Vulkan
presenter integration remains pending; Qt Quick's graphics-API selection must
not silently remove one of those qualified paths or add a CPU frame copy.

Qt 6.10.2's `QWaylandEglWindow::contentFBO()` creates a default-format content
FBO for decorated windows; its EGL window setup also requests 8-bit alpha when
decoration support is enabled. The native-child arrangement avoids that content
FBO rather than hiding the loss by logging only `QOpenGLContext::format()`.

Do not ship a global disable-decoration environment override, remove window
controls, enable HDR, or patch Qt silently to make this gate pass. The separate
`QT_WAYLAND_DISABLE_WINDOWDECORATION=1` diagnostic confirmed the direct-window
problem but is not part of the proposed design. The ordinary main window remains
unchanged. This is still an Ubuntu-only experiment, disabled in shipping builds.

Source references for this finding:

- https://github.com/qt/qtbase/blob/v6.10.2/src/plugins/platforms/wayland/plugins/hardwareintegration/wayland-egl/qwaylandeglwindow.cpp
- https://github.com/qt/qtbase/blob/v6.10.2/src/plugins/platforms/wayland/plugins/hardwareintegration/wayland-egl/qwaylandglcontext.cpp
- https://github.com/qt/qtbase/blob/v6.10.2/src/plugins/platforms/wayland/qwaylanddisplay.cpp

Reproduce component tests on the Ubuntu builder with
`PLANK_QT_SESSION_TEST_BUILD=<dedicated-shadow-build> scripts/test/run-qt-session-tests.sh`.
The standalone probe is built in that directory's `probe/` subdirectory. It
does not connect, capture microphone/camera/tablet input, or require credentials.
`scripts/test/run-qt-session-compositor.sh` runs a separate headless compositor
with a private runtime directory/session bus and in-memory settings; it never
attaches to the user's desktop. Set `PLANK_QT_CHECK_PRECISION=1` to include the
fail-closed native-child framebuffer and title-bar gates. The standalone probe's
`--precision-check` without `--native-child` remains the direct-window comparison;
it is expected to fail in decorated mode on the current Qt/GNOME combination.
Use `--native-child` for the proposed arrangement, and `--roundtrip` with a
precision check for repeated fullscreen/windowed transitions.

## Packed video and delivery checkpoint

Validated parent `5d19f9cd371a2e6c5ba7e9c3a2c2ba010dca77b3` / Client
`f816851f7f8088a397a1bfafd2083f0dedb750b2` adds `qtvideo.pri` to the isolated
probe, not the live Client session:

- `QtVaapiVideoFrame` retains the decoder's AVFrame while its exported DMA-BUF
  is sampled. It validates exact packed format, bit depth, range, matrix,
  object/plane layout and linear-buffer bounds. Surface synchronization is
  bounded to 100 ms on the decoder worker, never the Qt GUI thread.
- `QtEglVideoItem` imports that DMA-BUF into a scene-graph render node. At most
  three GPU leases are retained. Fences retire completed sampling without a
  per-frame `glFinish`, CPU pixel map or readback. Real Intel driver behavior,
  multi-output delivery and device-loss recovery are still pending gates.
- The shared packed-format interpretation is also used by the existing VA-API
  renderer, without changing its channel mapping. Other shipping renderer and
  transport paths remain intact.
- `QtVideoMailbox` coalesces frames to one pending frame and one queued GUI
  notification. Connection generations reject late callbacks; closing the
  session from a delivery is safe. A worker does not wait for GUI dispatch.

Twelve component checks pass normally and with ASan/UBSan/leak detection.
They use wrapped VA-API exports, not a real decoder/device. The separate shader
probe uses test-only CPU-generated GPU textures and readback; neither is part
of the proposed runtime. With `--video-precision-check`, all 1024 native RGB/gray
levels and crop/offset remain exact. Eight-bit expansion retains every original
code; legal adjacent-integer UNORM rounding is not mistaken for loss of source
precision. Full-range BT.709 gray and linear downscale also pass. An 8-bit
intermediate must fail the 10-bit gate.

All eight native-child framebuffer/shader configurations pass at 100% and forced
125% on the isolated compositor, including repeated fullscreen/windowed changes.
The same 24 input/window tests pass there and under sanitizers. This does not
qualify physical fractional-scale outputs, hardware decoding, GPU throughput or
live video/audio synchronization.

`run-qt-session-tests.sh` now needs `PLANK_CLIENT_FFMPEG_WORK` pointing to the
canonical patched private FFmpeg 9.0.1 tree. It verifies both patch application
and the qualified resulting source hash; do not bootstrap a second copy.
Use a separate shadow build with `PLANK_QT_SESSION_SANITIZERS=1`,
`ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=1` and `UBSAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=1` for sanitizers.
No ThreadSanitizer or Intel hardware acceptance is implied.

## Reusable software-frame pool checkpoint

Parent `cf34d49de312a6587c929fb9fdccc612c5e42860` / Client
`6e34e649a390bcb3a723edaa51f6a4399e40caa9` extracts the existing Vulkan
software-frame allocator into `PlVkFramePool`, with no Qt/SDL window dependency.
The normal renderer delegates to it; the Qt runtime is not connected yet.
The cacheable host-memory import policy and mapped/system fallbacks remain.
No extra CPU pixel upload or per-frame GPU finish is introduced.

An imported buffer must not return to FFmpeg for writing until its Vulkan plane
transfer completes. Completion callbacks only set an atomic flag: they may run
inside `pl_buf_create` while `av_buffer_pool_get` holds FFmpeg's pool mutex.
Actual AVBuffer release occurs outside GPU calls and allocator locks. Shutdown
drains GPU work before retiring pools, and a device-lifetime token lets external
AVFrame leases outlive the pool. The existing renderer uses its established
decoder-before-GPU destruction order. Future Qt teardown must run the drain on
its rendering owner, not the GUI thread.

Eighteen tests pass normally and under ASan/UBSan/leak detection. They include
forced delayed/reentrant completion, eight decoder threads, retained frame
clones, allocation/upload errors and malformed/inverted plane bounds. Six
tests perform actual software decoding of the existing H.264 8/10-bit 4:2:2 and
4:4:4 plus HEVC 8/10-bit identity fixtures, followed by Vulkan transfer/readback.
Every visible sample matches the system-memory reference decode. The test-frame
bytes are unchanged; they now live in shared `ffmpeg-testframes.h` instead of a
CPP included into the decoder. Fixtures and readback are qualification inputs,
not a runtime pixel-copy path. lavapipe explicitly serves this isolated test;
production must still qualify the real GPU and exact profile.

Both identity variants, 24 input/window and 12 delivery/lifetime checks still
pass under sanitizers. All eight scene/framebuffer/shader configurations still
pass at 100% and forced 125% in the isolated compositor. The ordinary renderer
and decoder translation units pass a syntax-only check. The earlier local
builder lacked PipeWire headers; the operator has now explicitly required
GitHub-hosted Actions instead. Its normal Client dependency installer already
includes those headers and the independently verified private SDL build. This does
not qualify Vulkan-to-Qt interop, real VA-API, live sessions or throughput.

Next remains decoder-to-Qt presentation (including explicit cross-API ownership),
then asynchronous Session integration. Do not enable packaging until those are
wired. See HANDOFF for current hosted validation state.

## Hosted software-conversion work

The `linux-client-qt-tests` dispatch target builds from the exact pushed branch
on Ubuntu 26.04 / Qt 6.10.2, reusing the normal verified Client bootstrap/cache.
It installs no product and uploads only isolated-test reports. Do not resume
builds on the retained Ubuntu VM for this experiment.
The job also compiles/links the complete ordinary Client with its existing
preflight tests because the extracted frame pool is shared with that renderer.
This regression binary is not packaged or uploaded as the experimental Client.
Software-only GPU selection is explicit; leak detection remains enabled.

`QtSoftwareVideoConverter` reuses the pooled decoder allocations, uploads the
planes without another CPU pixel copy, and performs full-range identity or
BT.709 conversion into an exact RGB10_A2 Vulkan image. The output must match the
decoded dimensions: Qt's final presenter owns scaling/cropping once. No HDR,
transfer-function conversion, dithering or silent 8-bit target is accepted.
Tests cover all RGB code values, channel order, full-range YCbCr gray/color,
actual encoded fixtures and rejection of format/range/size substitutions.
This conversion is not yet connected to the Qt scene or a network session.
Plane uploads explicitly select R8/R16 storage. The converter uses the decoded
bit depth for full-range normalization/neutral chroma and the same explicit
BT.709 matrix as the packed hardware path. The ordinary renderer retains its
existing automatic plane selection. The 19 conversion tests pass normally and
under ASan/UBSan with leak detection on the hosted software GPU, including odd
dimensions and inverted strides. The complete ordinary Client also compiles/links
and passes its standard preflight and runtime-dependency checks. See HANDOFF for
the exact source and hosted run; none of this establishes live Qt session or
Intel GPU acceptance.

The probe also inventories Vulkan/OpenGL external-memory and semaphore support
and compares device/driver identities without logging their UUIDs. A capability
report is not successful interop. Opaque handles must come from matching
devices/drivers; DMA-BUF export with an implicit modifier must not be assumed to
work across drivers. The eventual GPU handoff must explicitly synchronize both
directions without a per-frame CPU readback or global GPU finish.
The hosted Mesa software driver exposes external memory but no external
semaphores. Its framebuffer/conversion results therefore cannot qualify the
proposed cross-API handoff. Keep that gate distinct from component compilation.

Primary interop references:

- https://registry.khronos.org/OpenGL/extensions/EXT/EXT_external_objects.txt
- https://registry.khronos.org/OpenGL/extensions/EXT/EXT_external_objects_fd.txt
- https://github.com/haasn/libplacebo/blob/v7.360.0/src/include/libplacebo/vulkan.h

## Co-installation contract

| Resource | Ordinary Client | Qt experiment |
| --- | --- | --- |
| DEB / executable | `plank-client` | `plank-client-qt` |
| Display name | PLANK Client | PLANK Client Qt |
| Desktop ID | `la.instinctual.Plank.Client` | `la.instinctual.Plank.Client.Qt` |
| QSettings application | `PLANK` | `PLANK Client Qt` |
| Administrator policy | `/etc/plank/client.conf` | `/etc/plank/client-qt.conf` |
| Private libraries | `/usr/lib/plank` | `/usr/lib/plank-client-qt` |
| Logs below XDG state home | `plank/logs` | `plank-client-qt/logs` |

The distinct Qt application name also separates bookmarks, Host trust, caches
and application data. There is no automatic import or shared mutable state.
Portable-mode markers are ignored by the experiment. Both apps may be installed;
concurrent live streams using the same physical input devices are not qualified.
Both packages grant the same Wacom device access using separately owned udev
files. Removing either package must not remove the other's files or rules.

The `PLANK_CLIENT_VARIANT=qt` scaffolding selects the names and private RPATH;
default production naming is unchanged. The experimental package may not claim
`Conflicts`, `Replaces`, `Provides`, production URL handlers or autostart. The
package collector only accepts it as a branch-labelled Ubuntu candidate.
`scripts/test/check-client-qt-package.py` audits the built DEB and can compare
its file list with the actual production DEB before delivery.

All fourteen `tests/packaging/test-client-qt-package.py` checks pass on Ubuntu.
Its synthetic install/purge test uses a disposable dpkg root, verifies production
binary/library/configuration preservation and never installs product software on
the builder. Both compiled application-identity test variants pass and verify
separate bookmark/trust namespaces. The actual 1.1.043 candidate also passes
the payload comparison against the published 1.1.039 production DEB.

A source/linkage gate now requires the actual Qt lifecycle, renderer, input and
Wayland policy owners in the binary. It replaces the earlier blanket prohibition
once live-runtime integration was complete. Do not distribute a renamed SDL
Client or the non-networked probe as a completed Qt Client. The hosted
`linux-client-qt` target runs the complete qualification before building the
branch-labelled DEB under
`artifacts/packages/candidates/<version>-ubuntu-qt-session/linux/`.
No merge, release or remote installation is authorized by this testing request.

## Integrated candidate scope

The Qt session now uses the existing launch/authentication, takeover, media,
profile selection, reconnect and asynchronous teardown paths. Qt owns display
enumeration, windows, toolbar, cursor and keyboard/mouse dispatch; even launcher
decoder probing no longer initializes SDL video. Exact-format qualification
runs on the real session surface instead. SDL audio remains intentionally reused.

The complete toolbar includes bitrate acknowledgement, shared video/loss/RTT
telemetry, microphone/camera, stats, pin/drag, fullscreen, minimize and disconnect.
Keyboard shortcut policy and idle/pointer inhibition use Qt's Wayland connection.
Raw and normalized tablet readers retain their current recovery implementation.
Mouse input uses the same render viewport, including cross-output drags.
Primary-output presentation feedback continues driving the existing audio clock.

Hosted gates include real shader precision checks, P010/packed imports using
synthetic surfaces, all current exact-profile fixtures, generation/lease tests,
tablet-lifecycle test doubles, ordinary-Client regression builds and Qt launcher
smoke tests. They do not simulate a physical GPU or prove a real network session.
Hardware acceptance remains the next mandatory gate after candidate delivery.

Hosted run 39333288277 built and packaged parent
`fa9ff666fb1c9292ae19ff4711671be157e55777` / Client
`16f99202fde2942692d2acc5ac26bc96eda9aad7`. It passes 32 window/input cases
offscreen, at 100% and forced 125% on GNOME/Wayland, and under ASan/UBSan.
The new rendered test replaces the tablet cursor texture 24 times, then covers
clear/recreate, mouse/tablet visibility changes and final destruction. The node
alone owns and retires its textures; no manual pre-deletion is permitted before
`setTexture()`. Raw-HID forwarding and Host tablet lifetime are unchanged.
Startup/released-input/timeout Disconnect uses the same keyboard-layout
resolver as active-stream shortcuts. Three full launcher cycles pass with
SDL video deliberately unavailable. The separate DEB is in the normal package
catalog; it has not been deployed, merged, released or physically accepted.
The software runner cannot qualify Vulkan/GL external semaphore interop; that
test remains explicitly skipped rather than converted into a pass.
